<h2>Feeding the region's food-processing and aquaculture industries</h2>
<p>Southeast Asia's Indian import profile is shaped by what its major industries need: groundnut kernels for food processing (Indonesia is India's largest single market for this product), fish meal for large aquaculture sectors in Vietnam, and onion, chilli and spices for both direct culinary use and food manufacturing.</p>

<h2>Halal certification matters in two of the three markets</h2>
<p>Malaysia and Indonesia both have large Muslim-majority populations and formalised halal certification systems (JAKIM in Malaysia, BPJPH in Indonesia). Where halal certification is required, your importer specifies the certifying body and we arrange documentation to match — we don't make halal claims ourselves, and this is worth raising at the enquiry stage.</p>

<h2>Regional food-processing hubs</h2>
<p>Malaysia and Indonesia both serve as regional food-processing and re-distribution centres beyond their own domestic markets. If your buyer is processing or re-exporting, flag it — it can affect labelling and documentation requirements.</p>
